WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.052 --> 00:00:09.010
KEYUR GOVANDE: I'm the
chief architect at Etsy.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.590
And we're here to talk
about Etsy's migration

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.755
to Google Cloud.

00:00:12.755 --> 00:00:13.630
DANNY ROSEN: Awesome.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:14.130
Thanks.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.070
My name's Danny Rosen.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.780
I'm the technical program
manager at Google.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.930
I worked with Etsy on
their cloud migration,

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.750
which we're really excited
to talk to you about today.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.090
KEYUR GOVANDE: So a quick
introduction to what is Etsy.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.880
Etsy is the world's
largest marketplace

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.220
for unique and creative goods.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.180
We have a community that
has over 2 million sellers,

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.880
39 million buyers, and
we have 50 million items

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.680
for sale on the site.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.760
We're also heavily diverse.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.525
30% of our engineers are
women, four out of six execs

00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:48.250
are women, and we have
gender parity on the board.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.010
A lot of the Etsy
sellers are also women.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.870
For the vast majority
of them, Etsy

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.690
is their only source of income
and they're businesses of one.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.120
So we take all of
that into account

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.521
as we make decisions
for the company.

00:01:00.521 --> 00:01:03.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:04.760
Thank you.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.190
So a little bit of
background about Etsy.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.410
Etsy was founded in 2015--

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:10.910
sorry, in 2005.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.050
This was a time that the cloud
did not exist as a concept.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.730
We were solely in
the data center.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:21.590
And in that time and era, we had
to build a lot of our own tech,

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.310
because there was not much
out there that we could use.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:28.220
You may have heard of a little
daemon called StatsD that

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.890
was originally written by Etsy.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.050
We have a bunch of CI/CD
tooling that we have open

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.970
sourced like deploy nature.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.720
And we have a fairly popular
programming and engineering

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.130
blog at codeascraft.com.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.230
The reason that I bring
that up is that we

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.980
used to be data center focused.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.120
People who have worked in the
data center know this timeline.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.320
You budget three
to six months out.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.210
You order hardware.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.550
It takes some time to come in.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:58.400
Then you receive it.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.693
If you rack and stack it.

00:01:59.693 --> 00:02:00.610
Then you provision it.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.985
You set up all the software
that you need on it before it

00:02:02.985 --> 00:02:04.460
can finally enter production.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.890
The timeline is really long.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.789
And Etsy was finding that
this workflow was not scaling

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.690
as the site was growing.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.788
And primarily, these are
the three main benefits

00:02:17.788 --> 00:02:18.330
of the cloud.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.220
Like there is compute capacity,
elasticity, and efficiency.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.250
Etsy is trying-- the 50
million items that we

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.820
showed are completely unique.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.330
They're not SKUs that
we can easily search.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.490
So we needed a lot
of compute in order

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:34.870
to apply machine learning to
figure out, for example, what

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.190
are the items to show to
our sellers, what would

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.420
be the most compelling to them.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.670
This was really hard
to do it correctly,

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:49.330
and at scale, and with
efficiency in the data center.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.870
In Google Cloud, we can--
or in a cloud environment,

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.900
we can spin up
nodes really quickly

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.620
without having to spend up
front in purchasing the nodes

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.940
and getting them ready to go.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.300
And the other thing
is efficiency.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.650
For Etsy, the traffic
follows a pattern

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:09.230
that peaks in the day and
then slows down at night.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.550
We are burning a lot of compute
keeping those boxes running 24

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.170
by 7 in the data center.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.660
In a cloud environment,
again, this is not a problem.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.640
When we don't need the
nodes, we turn them off.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.580
It's efficient both from a
cost perspective and also

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.500
an environmental perspective.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.993
So why Google Cloud?

00:03:25.993 --> 00:03:27.410
DANNY ROSEN: It's
a good question.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:28.900
Why Google Cloud indeed?

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.630
So there are four major aspects
of why Etsy chose Google Cloud.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.130
The first is culture.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.660
Google's culture
and Etsy's culture

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.472
follow really interesting
connectivity that we're

00:03:38.472 --> 00:03:39.680
going to talk about in a sec.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.792
The second is there's
a focus on ML.

00:03:42.792 --> 00:03:44.750
If you haven't figured
that out already as part

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.292
of our marketing narrative, it's
really clear in the products

00:03:47.292 --> 00:03:49.850
that we're pushing out and
how Etsy is implementing it.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:50.920
It's pretty interesting.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.870
Finally, the second or the
third, international data

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:54.582
centers.

00:03:54.582 --> 00:03:56.540
There's a long list of
data centers we support.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.545
And it works for Etsy's
global footprint.

00:03:58.545 --> 00:04:00.920
And then finally, we both have
a really strong commitment

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.265
to sustainability,
making sure that we're

00:04:03.265 --> 00:04:04.640
taking care of
the Earth together

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.350
and making the right
decisions for ourselves.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:09.100
So we say culture.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.730
And what does that
actually mean?

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.650
On the board, we have
two different articles.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.660
The first is straight
out of the SRE book

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:19.295
about postmortem culture,
this idea of being blameless.

00:04:19.295 --> 00:04:22.730
A while ago, a person named
John Allspaw from Etsy

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:25.280
actually wrote one of the
major pieces of this in Code

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.930
as Craft that's
referenced even today.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.197
And when you look at blameless
and postmortem culture,

00:04:31.197 --> 00:04:33.030
you ask yourself, well,
what's the big deal?

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.173
Why is this important?

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:35.590
And it's about
iterative learning.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:35.840
Right.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.490
It's about, hey,
we made a mistake.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.170
And that's OK,
because we're thinking

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.100
for the long term
as a collective,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.402
rather than for the short
term for somebody to blame.

00:04:44.402 --> 00:04:45.860
The next thing is
machine learning.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:46.360
Right?

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.655
So finding the right crocheted
octopus on Etsy, kind of hard.

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:50.780
How are you going to do it?

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:51.590
Machine learning.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:52.160
Pretty clear.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.810
Notably there are 4,444
crocheted octopuses

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.280
on etsy.com.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.240
Totally not kidding.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.170
This is not an edit.

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.670
Like feel free.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:02.330
Yeah.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.810
So if you need a crochet
octopus, this is where you go.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.390
But Etsy's search engineering
team, also represented

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:11.330
in the room today,
is actively looking

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.210
at different ways of providing
good search results that

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.440
are relevant to queries
that users are putting in.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.440
So searching for the word
couch really brings up

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.630
a couch, not a
pillow of a couch.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.252
That's the idea.

00:05:22.252 --> 00:05:23.960
Next thing is
international data centers.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:24.460
Right.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.290
And there's so many on the
board and maybe one day

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.190
a whole lot more as we grow.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.540
But this falls in line
with Etsy's global plans,

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.270
Google's plans of expanding
and having good partners that

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:36.830
are tech-centric
and building out

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.670
in these different locations.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.460
And then finally,
I think the thing

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.750
that is a little more
emotional but is relevant,

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.580
like I have a family.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.583
And I'd like that family to
grow one day and to persist.

00:05:49.583 --> 00:05:52.250
And if we take care of the Earth
and we're doing the right thing

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.230
sustainably with these big data
centers using a ton of power,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.957
then we're going to make sure
that my family and your family

00:05:57.957 --> 00:05:59.790
are going to have a
better place to live in.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.770
And I think that as
a community in tech,

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:06.530
as we grow, the thing
that we're using is power.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:07.990
We do so sustainably
and we think

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:11.280
about the broader ecosystem.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.913
So that's why Google.

00:06:12.913 --> 00:06:14.330
Let's talk a bit
about how we made

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.820
this work and the partnership
between the two teams.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:22.160
So it began in December
of 2017 in New York City

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.320
in Eric Schmidt's side office.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.000
There was a business
conversation

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.750
between lawyers, salespeople,
talking about how

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.000
we're going to make this work.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.260
What's the contract look like?

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.810
And I'm sitting there
chewing on my fingernails

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:33.840
like, I don't know.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:34.340
Right?

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.885
And the right people
in the room were

00:06:35.885 --> 00:06:37.010
asking the right questions.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:38.030
And we made it work.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.270
We started our partnership
on that day in December.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.160
Weeks later, suddenly
I find myself

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.200
embedded as part of Etsy's
team in their Slack,

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.450
answering questions, talking
about different technical

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:50.360
approaches to building things
out, and actually working out

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.070
of their Brooklyn
office once a week.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:56.000
Notably, Etsy has probably the
best coffee in Brooklyn hands

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.980
down.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.530
So from there, we start to
understand the challenges

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:01.400
or problems.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.100
As I'm embedded, I become
part of the culture.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.720
And we start doing hackathons.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:07.850
One of the teams was
blocked on some deeper

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.043
details of Kubernetes.

00:07:09.043 --> 00:07:11.710
And we're able to say, OK, let's
bring you into a Google office,

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:14.330
get some Google subject matter
experts on the floor with you,

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.077
and actually do a
Kubernetes hackathon.

00:07:16.077 --> 00:07:18.410
And in doing so we unblock
the tremendous amount of work

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.410
from their backlog that
they had concerns about,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.090
because they simply didn't have
the background or experience.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.080
So we were able to knock
that out right away.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.330
There are some other really
interesting notable things

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:30.380
like Seth Vargo, who is one
of our developer advocates,

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.710
did a tech talk on Terraform.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.390
And he did it at Etsy.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.700
And it was so well done
that it is now literally

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:40.625
a piece of Etsy's
onboarding education

00:07:40.625 --> 00:07:43.250
for their new engineers so that
they know how to use Terraform.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:44.705
It was a Q&amp;A session.

00:07:44.705 --> 00:07:47.330
Seth came and really provided a
tremendous amount of assistance

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:52.680
in depth, because Seth
used to work at HashiCorp.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:53.570
Yeah.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.927
And then finally, we
did a lot of engineering

00:07:55.927 --> 00:07:57.510
and organizational
cultural alignment.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.610
So I mean like the cultures
are tech companies.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.360
At the end of the day,
we like technology.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:03.460
We like building solutions.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:05.002
We like figure out
the right problem.

00:08:05.002 --> 00:08:06.980
So there's a lot of
good alignment there.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.250
KEYUR GOVANDE: So
like Danny said,

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.540
signed the contract
in December of 2017.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:20.070
And we had a hard deadline for
shutting down our data centers

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:21.840
about 20 months away.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.270
This was more or less like
a leadership decision.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:25.860
We did not want to pay.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.388
We had signed a
commit with Google.

00:08:27.388 --> 00:08:28.680
We had a data center footprint.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:32.120
And we did not want to have an
extremely long migration where

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:36.090
we're paying for two things
and doing things slowly

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:38.640
and iteratively over n years.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.159
We felt that we could
move a little bit faster.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:45.240
And I'll explain more of that
as we go through the talk.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:51.750
So we essentially had 20 months,
but four of those 20 months

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.450
are the busiest period of Etsy.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:54.640
It's the holiday season.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:56.880
Etsy's a shopping platform.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.430
And that is our biggest quarter.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.490
We try not to make large
infrastructural changes then

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.720
just to avoid instability
for our users.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.388
So preferably, we would
prefer that the large portion

00:09:09.388 --> 00:09:11.430
of the migration finish
in the first eight months

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.340
so that we have the other eight
months as a fallback in case

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:19.140
we need to fix some things or
have a complete do over even.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.680
So just to remind you
of the ground rules

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.010
that we set for ourselves, no
large architectural changes.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:31.320
This is primarily because
we felt that doing a rewrite

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:34.110
would add a tremendous amount
of risk to the project.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.930
Etsy, like I said before,
has been around since 2005.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.960
The code base has a
lot of kinks in it,

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.670
its legacy if you would
be OK calling that.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:49.060
And we did not feel
that we had the time

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:53.760
to rewrite it into a
microservices architecture

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.000
in a way that would
stick with our timelines.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.440
The other thing was to migrate
the fewer systems needed.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:01.940
Etsy has been around
for many years.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.620
We have lots of systems, a
lot of supporting systems.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.020
And we wanted to see what is
the minimum set of systems

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:10.440
that we can migrate in order
to run etsy.com in GCP.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:13.560
And thirdly, to stay compliant,
we're a public company,

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.800
we had to comply with a set
of regulations and rules

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.800
that we could not break.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.890
So this is roughly the
timeline that we came up with.

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:26.400
Start the partnership,
set up a squad,

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.080
do all the work to get there,
and then cut over by August

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:32.610
and then do some tuning.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:36.030
If everything goes smoothly,
then by the time Thanksgiving

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:39.720
hits, the site is running in
GCP and is completely stable.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:43.210
So Danny briefly touched
on the partnership aspect

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:45.480
so I'm just going to skip
forward to the migration.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.960
So Etsy's infrastructure is
made up of a bunch of teams.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.050
They own their own systems,
their own roadmaps,

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.060
and their own tools.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.360
And this creates a problem
for a project as complicated

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.120
as a migration, because the
migration throws a wrench

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.980
into everyone's plans.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:06.025
We did not want to
disrupt all of these teams

00:11:06.025 --> 00:11:08.400
from doing what they were
doing, which was really mission

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.380
critical stuff for etsy.com.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:14.160
So instead what we came up
with was a migration squad.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.250
We took one or more people
from every one of those teams,

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.800
we took away all of their
other responsibilities,

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.880
and focused them solely on
this task of moving etsy.com

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:24.990
to the cloud.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:31.200
This allowed them to basically
not get distracted by the day

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:31.913
to day stuff.

00:11:31.913 --> 00:11:34.080
Etsy.com was still running
in the data center, which

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.780
meant that there were still
things that needed to be fixed,

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.570
issues that came up, site
outages and stuff like that.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.820
But they did not have to
focus on any of those things.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:45.330
They were solely working
on the migration.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.520
So once we had the
squad set up, we

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:51.660
had to figure out how to work
with the tools in the cloud.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.630
Etsy, as I had
alluded to before,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:59.130
had a bunch of custom in-house
tooling to manage things

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:00.130
in the data center.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:03.120
For example, to provision a
box, we wrote our own tools.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.760
It is open sourced, but
it was written by us

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.100
to manage the
various permutations

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:09.600
and combinations of hardware.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.760
In Google Cloud, this
was not a problem.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:12.850
We had to pick a tool.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.690
We picked Terraform, but we
had to learn how to use it.

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.720
Google was, again,
a great partner.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.770
Seth Vargo came on
board, taught us

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:22.890
a lot about how to correctly
do Terraform at scale.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:26.760
But it was something that we
had to learn and figure out.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.840
Another example of this would be
learning how to use Stackdriver

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:34.600
or learning the ins and
outs of the Google network,

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.300
if you will.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.973
So just to segue a little bit
into the Etsy architecture,

00:12:40.973 --> 00:12:42.390
we've been around
for a long time.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:43.960
We're a LAMP stack.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:47.400
That was the hottest
thing to do in 2005.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:50.480
Linux, Apache, PHP, MySQL.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:52.650
And because we've been
around for so long,

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:54.870
we have a giant monolith.

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:58.350
It's held together by
our monolithic shards.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.080
And then memcache is our
primary caching tier.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.740
And the code is all
sorts of tangles,

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.060
which are really
hard to figure out

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:11.270
and sometimes
causes some issues.

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.820
But the good thing about
the Etsy architecture

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.100
is that the systems diagram
is relatively straightforward.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.780
There is a load balancer upfront
that serves requests for--

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.670
that processes the first--

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:25.380
that is the first stop
in a request to etsy.com.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.220
There's some web servers
that field the request.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:30.380
They further talk
to some web servers

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:33.110
hidden behind some internal load
balancers eventually talking

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:34.350
to a database.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.770
This is an architecture
that we were

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:39.080
able to reason about really
well about the performance

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:44.010
characteristics, the sort
of what could go wrong.

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:47.030
A premortem was really
easy or relatively

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:51.140
easy to do with this kind
of architecture that we had.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.840
Thing over time that has
been happening at Etsy

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:56.900
is we've been also doing
API first development.

00:13:56.900 --> 00:13:59.150
Like I said before,
we're a PHP site

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:01.820
so we don't really have
the idea of an event loop,

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:05.240
but we've emulated that
by using [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.860
So let me quickly
explain how that works.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.880
So this is-- if you think
of the etsy.com homepage,

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:16.360
there's a bunch of modules on
it, so like a favorites module,

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:20.240
a friends activity, or Etsy
blog content, or promotions,

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.860
or personalized content for you.

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:24.920
So when you land on
etsy.com, it actually

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:28.160
splits up internally
into four requests

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:30.410
for all of the different
modules that I described,

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:32.570
which may further break
up into more requests

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:36.260
to actually fetch the data
from the cache or a database.

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:39.800
And all of these are API
tiers that are hidden away.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:41.780
The nice aspect of
this is it allows

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:45.380
us to scale the system
at every single layer

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:46.880
without affecting
the other layer,

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.090
so every single one of these
is a web server and a load

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.920
balancer so we're able to
scale up and down as we need

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:55.730
and reason about the system
without needing to understand

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:00.140
the full picture or having
to worry about dependencies

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:07.700
or a tangled tree of code.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:12.340
So how on earth do we migrate
this thing to Google Cloud?

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:17.320
Just to quickly reiterate,
these were our goals.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.900
And the way that
we talked about it

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:23.380
internally was like maybe we
can set up some read replicas.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:26.270
Etsy.com, simple application,
why wouldn't a read replica

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:26.770
work?

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:32.030
We discussed this internally
for a very long time,

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:33.730
but it came down
to the fact that,

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:35.660
because of our
monolithic shards,

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.650
we had come to rely
on some behaviors

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:41.710
like immediately read
after write consistency.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:43.990
This would be really
hard to replicate

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.870
if we were to spin up replicas
of our data center setup

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:51.940
in GCP that would be many
tens of milliseconds away

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:53.680
from the data center,
which would mean

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.990
that their consistency
would not be

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:57.970
accurate to within a
millisecond or even

00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:01.012
within a single transaction.

00:16:01.012 --> 00:16:02.470
Another thing that
we discussed was

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.440
to maybe spin up
active active, so keep

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:07.660
one copy of etsy.com
running in the data center

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:10.150
and then spin up a
completely active copy in GCP

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:15.040
in multiple regions and redirect
traffic maybe over like GOIP

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.970
based on where the
user is coming from.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:20.260
But again, the monolith and
the tangled dependencies

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:21.940
made this really
hard for us to do

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:23.620
without having to do a rewrite.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.570
And one of our key
goals was to not do

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.310
a rewrite of the application.

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:31.480
So what we ended up doing,
or what we landed on,

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:34.330
was a sort of lift and shift.

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:36.670
As I said before,
like the application

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:39.253
we felt we could reason
about reasonably well.

00:16:39.253 --> 00:16:41.920
So a lift and shift was actually
a perfectly reasonable approach

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:43.540
for where we were in time.

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:46.390
Remember we only had
20 months to migrate.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:47.950
And our application
was a monolith

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:51.380
that we did not feel we
could rewrite in that period.

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:54.130
But the problem with
the lift and shift

00:16:54.130 --> 00:17:00.160
was that there is no clean
way to switch traffic over.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:01.780
If you had an
active active copy,

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:04.900
you could bring all of them up
and then turn one of them down.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:06.150
No one would notice.

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.960
But with a lift and shift,
that was really not an option.

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:12.013
So we instead proactively
communicated with our sellers

00:17:12.013 --> 00:17:13.930
that we're going to take
a maintenance window.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:17.170
The site will be unavailable
for a portion of time

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:21.099
while we migrated to Google
Cloud and brought it up there.

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:25.060
This is not, again, anyone's
first preferred approach.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:28.060
But we felt that given the
constraints that we were under

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:31.570
and basically time system
limitations that this

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:33.100
was the best approach for Etsy.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:34.780
And being proactive
with our sellers

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.760
meant that we did
not necessarily

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:37.760
cause them any surprise.

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.190
Like we didn't just go down.

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:41.260
We gave them enough
notice that this

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:45.330
was going to happen so they
were able to plan for it.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.582
So once we got all
of the tooling,

00:17:48.582 --> 00:17:50.540
once we got comfortable
with all of the tooling

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:53.660
and getting things
spun up in the cloud,

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:55.670
we entered the functional
testing and the load

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:58.040
and performance testing phase.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:01.340
So etsy.com is a
website driven primarily

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:03.780
by load in the holiday season.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.740
So we had some expectations
about that load.

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:10.190
We made sure to test for at
least 2x the traffic in GCP

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:14.120
once we had all of the
systems up and running.

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:16.460
And basically, even if
you're in the cloud,

00:18:16.460 --> 00:18:19.880
things that you focus on in
the data center still matter.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:21.440
For example, CP architectures.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:24.230
Like in the DC, all of our
boxes were really custom.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.930
We had them tuned really
well for our work load.

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:29.930
And we found that we had to do
some of that in GCP as well.

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:33.650
GCP offers, at
least at the time,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.750
three classes of CPU hardware.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.660
And we found that Skylake was
the most performant for us.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:41.980
But it was not the default in
the region that we were in.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:43.730
So as we did load and
performance testing,

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:46.260
we found this but we were
again able to tune it

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:49.800
with Terraform to always
pick the one that we needed.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:54.330
And it was a pretty
decent 10% win

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:57.380
in, sorry, the capacity for us.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:01.950
And at scale, even 99.9%
might not be enough.

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:05.210
So the Google global load
balancer at that time

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:09.620
had a bug where at extremely
long tail of requests,

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.980
the latency would spike a lot
because of some discovery.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:16.850
Google was a fantastic partner
who worked with us on this.

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:19.740
We told them we had an issue.

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:20.660
They engaged us.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:22.580
They set up all of
the packet captures

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:24.620
at the various levels
of the hypervisor

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:27.570
to figure this bug
out and fixed it.

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:30.230
But this was, again,
like a thing that

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.053
was a learning curve for Etsy.

00:19:32.053 --> 00:19:33.470
We were always in
the data center.

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:36.020
We knew we owned everything
so once that request

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.300
entered our network,
we had full control.

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:40.910
And this was something
that we had to adjust to.

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:42.380
If there was an
issue, how do you

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.080
work with your partner to
figure out what the issue is?

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:48.230
And how do you remediate that?

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:53.450
So a quick segue into the
most amazing outage probably

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:54.530
of my career.

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:58.070
So while we were
setting up Google Cloud

00:19:58.070 --> 00:19:59.960
and a parallel infrastructure
in Google Cloud

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:02.400
to do so to do all
of this testing,

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:07.670
we had staged the IP in
preparation for the cut over.

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:10.220
The way that we had done it
was we have two DNS providers.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:13.220
One of them supports
a do not serve flag.

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:14.930
Basically what that
means is never serve

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.190
this DNS record no matter what.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.450
But the other DNS provider did
not support a do not serve flag

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.450
so instead we added it
on into the provider

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:26.690
with a ratio of zero.

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:29.600
What zero means is
run health checks.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:34.800
And if the other
IP address is down,

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:38.060
then you're OK
serving this record.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:40.550
As it happens, after
we had done this,

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:42.470
etsy.com had a small outage.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:44.150
For about 10 minutes,
the main site

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:46.550
was down because we
we're doing something.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:47.870
It didn't work.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:52.490
But what happened was we served
the GCP test infrastructure IP

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:54.590
to our customers.

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:57.410
The good thing was that
that infrastructure was not

00:20:57.410 --> 00:21:01.610
scaled to handle traffic at the
rate at which the traffic had

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:03.660
landed, so it
immediately fell over.

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:05.240
But for a few of
our customers they

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:07.670
saw a slightly
unusual experience,

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:10.220
like the colors were are all
wrong because we were using

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:13.680
blue to signify that this is
a test infrastructure, not

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:14.880
the Etsy orange.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:21.140
So it caused some emails
to our support site.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:24.920
But in like true Etsy fashion,
we celebrated this outage.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:29.510
It was completely unexpected
behavior for everyone involved.

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:32.870
And the engineer who
was most proximate to it

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:35.270
won the Etsy's coveted
three-armed sweater

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:39.260
award for causing the most
spectacular outage of the year.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:46.690
So moving on the
timeline finally coming

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:48.490
to the actual cut
over, we did all

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:51.430
of the functional
testing, load testing,

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:52.990
in order to gain confidence.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:54.400
And we realized
that the only way

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.500
that we would know if
this would truly work

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:57.970
was to send live traffic at it.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:01.000
No amount of testing can mimic
the complexity of production.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:03.280
And the Etsy app
is complex enough

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:06.040
that we could not cover
every single thing

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:07.640
through simulation.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:10.070
So we felt that we had
to at least cut over

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:15.460
once to learn more about what
are the hardest problems here

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:19.930
that we have not found basically
that our engineers may not even

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:21.550
be aware of.

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:26.140
So the way that we planned for
it was to have a soft release.

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:28.270
This was a new thing for Etsy.

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:30.190
We are a continuous
deployment shop.

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:32.420
We deploy 30 times a day.

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.900
But in preparation
for cut over, we

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:36.400
communicated to all
of our engineers

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:41.020
that for a couple of days
things will be moving slower,

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.600
so it would be a soft release
where only P1 [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:47.170
would go out and then we would
enter a full freeze where

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:52.290
no changes except those
required to basically set up

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:55.030
the cut over for
success would be allowed

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:57.300
and then there would be
a period of heightened

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:58.450
on call monitoring.

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:00.610
A lot of teams had
signed up for a rotation

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:02.860
so that they would help
observe the site once we

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:06.380
were running in GCP.

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:07.130
DANNY ROSEN: Yeah.

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:10.650
So that was all Etsy's approach
to the cut over process,

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:11.800
a pretty different one.

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:13.690
Google also had an approach.

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:15.370
So I think the
important context here

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:18.400
is that Etsy ran their own
data centers, everything

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:19.700
in their data centers.

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:21.360
So if any network
issues happened,

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.890
they had network engineers
that knew what was going on.

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:25.482
Now Etsy is taking
the responsibility

00:23:25.482 --> 00:23:27.190
of effectively running
their data centers

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:28.930
and giving it to Google.

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:30.130
That's scary.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:32.620
So what we did to
support them is we

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.680
went in and masqueraded
effectively as Etsy engineers.

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:40.030
And we went through a two week
immersed training in Etsy.

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:41.650
Myself and a couple
of other Googlers

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:43.690
went in and then said,
OK, we understand

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:45.640
the lay of the land,
how the systems work.

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.710
We provided what we're
just calling artifacts.

00:23:47.710 --> 00:23:48.210
Right.

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.980
We actually created
system overviews,

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:53.830
product overviews, and gave
that to our support teams

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:58.120
that they had context on
what to know if Etsy called

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:01.440
and something was broken,
specifically for cut over but,

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.470
hey, even after they
still have these artifacts

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:05.260
so they have some
system archeology

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:06.892
and knowledge of state.

00:24:06.892 --> 00:24:08.350
Another thing that's
important here

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:10.270
is that Etsy has an
interesting engineering

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:12.700
culture and Etsyan terms.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.140
And every company
has corp speak,

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:15.848
so we got that down
to you in a glossary.

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:21.210
So that's one part
of the work we did.

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:23.160
The other part was
we created what

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.590
we call the war
room, which basically

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:32.370
was a cut over day team approach
to connecting the Etsy team,

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:35.520
us, who were actually on
site at 3:00 in the morning

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:37.710
and the support teams
that were monitoring.

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:40.380
A couple days before,
myself, a couple of others,

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:44.130
were on a gigantic email
thread to hundreds of Googlers

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:47.790
saying Etsy is going live
this weekend at 3:00 AM.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:49.110
Effectively, be ready.

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:52.560
SRE teams, our product
teams, our engineering teams

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:55.090
all pretty much had an
awareness of the situation.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:57.480
And we did it.

00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:59.460
We got on site.

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:00.670
We came in numbers.

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:01.890
There were three of us.

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:03.340
It was 3:00 in the morning.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:06.112
And we worked together to
start the cut over process.

00:25:06.112 --> 00:25:08.070
Probably one of the most
important factors here

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:10.025
was that we had donuts.

00:25:10.025 --> 00:25:12.150
In having donuts, we were
able to energize the team

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:13.410
so I wanted to
thank specifically

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:15.460
the sales team at Google
for providing the donuts.

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:16.002
Thanks, guys.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:19.950
KEYUR GOVANDE: Cool.

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:21.490
So cut over day.

00:25:21.490 --> 00:25:23.850
We picked a Sunday morning.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:25.140
It was 3:00 AM.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:28.800
It was determined that
this was the lowest traffic

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:30.810
time of the week for us.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:34.200
It would be the least impactful
to do a cut over at this time

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:38.220
because we had planned on being
down for about four hours.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.440
So the rough idea
was start at 3:00 AM,

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:42.930
turn off etsy.com
in the data center,

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.610
do some validation and
testing in GCP on the info

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:49.800
that we have there, and then
slowly ramp up traffic to GCP

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:52.920
and then make a decision
about whether to stay or not.

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:57.000
As you can imagine, turning
off a website is not trivial.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:00.810
So etsy.com has been like a
continuously operated site

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.250
for many years.

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:05.820
And there are a lot of systems
that are always running

00:26:05.820 --> 00:26:07.050
to make everything smooth.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:11.370
So like offline processing jobs,
asynchronous jobs, big data

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:14.220
jobs, database backups,
all of these things

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:16.350
that we had to plan
for and execute

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:20.100
in a particular-- like in
an exact series of steps

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.665
in order to do it safely.

00:26:21.665 --> 00:26:23.040
Because we obviously
did not want

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:25.380
to lose any of our customer
information, nor did

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:29.610
we want our customers to see
weird behavior on the site.

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:32.130
So the way that we did
it, we blocked them off

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:35.700
at the CDN layer so that
everyone who's coming

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:36.990
sees a uniform experience.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:39.570
That the Etsy site is
down for maintenance,

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:43.380
and then revert to their
databases, their DBAs,

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:46.110
and other SRE-ish
roles to make sure

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.690
that everything turns off
in just the right order.

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:49.410
And we did this.

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.197
It took us about
an hour and a half,

00:26:51.197 --> 00:26:52.530
exactly what we had planned for.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:57.600
But the site was done
correctly and all of the data,

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:00.420
more importantly, was replicated
to GCP so that we could

00:27:00.420 --> 00:27:03.030
bring the site back up in GCP.

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:06.660
So around 4:30 AM
we did a cutover.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:10.320
The site was running in GCP,
but only for internal users,

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:13.530
only for all of our engineers
who were there to test.

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:16.950
And as these things happen,
we ran into something

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:18.450
that we had never
run into testing.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:25.470
We ran out of memory on some
key offline processing systems

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:27.660
that we had not
specifically tested.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:30.660
But they were important enough,
as we found during the cutover,

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:35.070
that we could not basically
launch to our real users.

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:37.560
It was also like
4:30 in the morning.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:40.170
No one had slept for the night,
everyone was really groggy,

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:42.870
and we felt that we
could not figure this out

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:45.480
in time to make our window.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:48.600
So, unfortunately,
we rolled back.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:53.160
But this was good from the way
that we approached the problem

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:54.660
because we discovered
something new.

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:57.480
We discovered that
we had not covered,

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:00.990
in enough detail, an
area of the site that we

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:04.415
needed to do better in
order to try this again.

00:28:04.415 --> 00:28:05.790
And why did we
have to try again?

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:07.020
Because of this.

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:09.240
We had eight months
to do this right.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.970
We were already in August and
if we didn't do it in August

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:14.430
then we would have to wait
four months, until January,

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.920
before we were able to
have another shot at this.

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:21.060
So we felt like that if we were
able to address the problems

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:24.720
that we saw, that we needed to
try again, at least once, so

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:27.308
that all of the people who
had worked really hard,

00:28:27.308 --> 00:28:29.850
their work would pay off without
having to wait for n months.

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:32.190
Those n months could mean
many different things,

00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:33.965
like code would have
changed in that time.

00:28:33.965 --> 00:28:35.340
Infrastructure in
the data center

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:36.780
would have drifted in that time.

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:40.140
And we would have had to sort
of redo a whole bunch of things

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:42.390
if we waited that long.

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:46.290
So after fixing and doing
a bunch more testing,

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:48.810
we decided to give ourselves
another shot at the cutover.

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:50.640
We made a couple of changes.

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:54.175
We decided to start earlier in
the night, so start at 1:00 AM.

00:28:54.175 --> 00:28:55.800
And we gave ourselves
a six hour window

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:59.460
to do the migration, rather than
the four hours from last time.

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:02.340
We felt that this
added enough buffer

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:05.560
that if we run into issues, that
we would be able to fix them.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:06.990
So we turned off etsy.com.

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:09.670
We had gotten really
good at this point,

00:29:09.670 --> 00:29:14.230
having done it twice, like once
off and then once on again.

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:18.780
And we ended up finding new
errors in the validation

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:19.380
testing phase.

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:24.450
As an example, run some daemons
that had too few threads.

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:26.790
And this was causing
some backups, some delays

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:28.370
in critical operations.

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:29.850
We fixed that.

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:32.550
In another case we
had too many threads.

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:35.010
And this was, again,
causing system instability.

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:37.890
But because we had set
ourselves enough time,

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:41.880
we were able to find and
remedy these on the spot.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:44.040
And finally, this
time around, we

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:46.800
were able to ramp up
real users to the site.

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:50.070
So around like 5:30 AM real
users in a slow trickle

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:54.060
started to see
etsy.com work in GCP.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:55.620
At which point it became--

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:58.560
after we had been running
in GCP for a few hours, this

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.420
was sort of a key decision,
which is, should we stay?

00:30:01.420 --> 00:30:03.960
We were learning new
things, as I will show

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.570
in some slides that follow.

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:08.570
But do we stay or
do we roll back?

00:30:08.570 --> 00:30:11.400
Are things good enough that
we can stay here and continue

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:12.000
to learn?

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:15.480
Or are they not good
enough on some axis

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:16.680
that we need to roll back?

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:18.360
The axis that we had
set for ourselves

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:21.600
were operational stability,
whether it was burning out

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:25.680
our ops teams, or is it
impacting our sales too much

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:30.060
in a way that we can measure,
or is the performance on GCP not

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:31.130
that great.

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:32.820
The performance
one is interesting

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:37.170
because, like I had said
before in my talk, in our data

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:41.826
center everything was really
tuned for optimal performance.

00:30:41.826 --> 00:30:44.683
We had the exact set of
hardware that would give us

00:30:44.683 --> 00:30:47.100
the best performance for our
databases, for our memcaches,

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:48.210
for our web servers.

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:51.460
And in GCP, there
is less variability,

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.670
like you only have a
certain class of boxes

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:55.510
and certain classes of CPUs.

00:30:55.510 --> 00:30:59.443
And we felt like we did a
lot of synthetic testing,

00:30:59.443 --> 00:31:01.860
but that can never capture
the complexity of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:04.140
We only covered
80% of the traffic.

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:07.040
And the other 20%, the
long tail, we had no idea--

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.150
or we had some idea,
but not an exact idea

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:11.020
of how that would perform.

00:31:11.020 --> 00:31:13.200
So it was like a
big variable for us.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.050
But we found that the
performance is actually

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:16.800
quite all right.

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:20.390
So all the way on the right
is performance in GCP.

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:24.090
The two blank spots in the
middle are two cutover windows.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:26.790
But as you can see, the
perf was not that bad.

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:29.823
It was within bounds
that we had set ourselves

00:31:29.823 --> 00:31:31.740
that this would be OK
and we can tolerate this

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:33.580
and we can make this
better over time,

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:37.620
but we'll continue
running in GCP.

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:41.430
So now we entered the
sort of tuning phase

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:45.150
before the big Black
Friday, Cyber Monday.

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:49.290
There was a ton of things that
we were discovering in GCP,

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:50.590
in production.

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:53.460
So optimizing system calls.

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:56.730
GCP, and any cloud provider,
is a massively virtualized

00:31:56.730 --> 00:31:58.020
environment.

00:31:58.020 --> 00:32:00.420
In the data center we were
running on bare metal.

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:03.840
We found that if we optimized
away some system calls,

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:06.540
it would make our performance
on some key systems

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:10.950
really predictable, like really
smooth and not as jagged.

00:32:10.950 --> 00:32:12.210
So we did that.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:16.650
The other key win for
us was, MySQL in GCP

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:20.850
had some really unusual
behavior on new mutexes which

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:23.820
was only visible when there
was sufficient amount of read

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:26.280
and write traffic to
this, that we were simply

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:29.010
unable to replicate
through synthetic testing

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:31.450
but that showed
up in production.

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:35.880
And we worked with Google
engineers and internal DBAs

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:38.880
and other systems-minded folks
to figure this out and fix it

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:44.250
by adjusting kernel parameters
and MySQL variables.

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:46.860
The other thing is,
again, the network

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.200
showed up as a huge factor.

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.990
Google's network is a
software defined network.

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:54.210
It is not routers and
switches in the way

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:55.800
that it would be
in a data center.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:33:02.140
And at extremely large
percentiles, 99.999%,

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:04.860
if you do it often
enough and at scale then

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:08.560
those network jitter slash
variances really matter.

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:12.240
So in the case of memcache, for
example, which we use really

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:14.670
heavily, we found
that the latency

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:16.950
would spike from
a few microseconds

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:18.900
to many hundreds
of milliseconds.

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:22.212
And this would matter
for our application.

00:33:22.212 --> 00:33:23.670
This was another
thing that we just

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:26.520
had to go to prod
to discover because

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:28.020
like the sort of
request patterns

00:33:28.020 --> 00:33:31.590
that prod has was really hard
to mimic in a test environment,

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:33.360
in a simulation.

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:37.860
But again, Google were
great partners with us.

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:39.990
We engaged them, they did
all the packet captures,

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:43.630
they figured out what was the
issue over some period of time,

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:44.500
and they fixed it.

00:33:44.500 --> 00:33:49.867
It was actually a bug in their
SDN that we found for them.

00:33:49.867 --> 00:33:50.700
DANNY ROSEN: Thanks.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:55.680
KEYUR GOVANDE: So this
is roughly the time line.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:57.150
We got through all
the tuning phase

00:33:57.150 --> 00:34:01.380
and we made it to Cyber
Monday without any issues,

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:02.865
like we survived in slush.

00:34:02.865 --> 00:34:05.910
More than survived, we were
able to scale up and down

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:09.600
during Cyber Monday
as we needed to

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:14.040
in order to manage all of the
load that was on the site.

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:17.530
And it worked out the
way that we had hoped.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:21.020
So some observations
from being in the Cloud.

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:24.409
We've been running in the Cloud
for about seven months now

00:34:24.409 --> 00:34:27.800
and we've learned some things.

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:30.230
So long-lived flows.

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:32.600
In the data center,
again, this was something

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:34.280
that we did not
have to care about.

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:37.639
If a service opened a
connection to another service

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:41.449
it could go on for
weeks, hours, months,

00:34:41.449 --> 00:34:43.350
and it would not matter.

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:46.670
But in Google software-defined
network this was a problem.

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:49.070
A connection can only
be idle for 10 minutes

00:34:49.070 --> 00:34:51.409
before the SDN kills it.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:54.110
This was something that we had
to discover the hard way, where

00:34:54.110 --> 00:34:57.980
we had a bunch of services
that were acting weirdly.

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:00.740
But it was something that we
could adopt to, like using

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:04.190
[INAUDIBLE] or using pings
allowed us to work around this.

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:06.890
But this was a thing
that we had to learn

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:11.480
and all of the service owners
had to adopt specifically to.

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:15.030
On the plus side, live
migrations, really good.

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:19.730
If Google needs to do
maintenance on a host,

00:35:19.730 --> 00:35:23.210
they will live migrate
you off to another host.

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:26.870
And this works even for things
like memcache and MySQL,

00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:30.680
which have large memory
pools and a lot of traffic.

00:35:30.680 --> 00:35:31.940
And the blip is really tiny.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:36.780
So like this is something
that made our jobs really easy

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:40.460
from an operational perspective.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:43.400
And lastly, infinite capacity.

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:47.780
The Cloud promises to be
infinite, but at scale

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:48.920
it really is not.

00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:49.670
DANNY ROSEN: Yeah.

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:50.380
KEYUR GOVANDE: What
do you think, Danny?

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:51.920
DANNY ROSEN: So I
can speak to that.

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:54.530
So I think that's part of the
narrative of Cloud, right.

00:35:54.530 --> 00:35:55.700
Which is, oh, I can
just burst and I

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:57.290
have all this capacity
and everything I need

00:35:57.290 --> 00:35:59.330
and all the CPUs I need and
all the RAM and everything

00:35:59.330 --> 00:36:00.020
is there for me.

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:01.610
Right?

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:03.920
So that's the Cloud
vision, all right?

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:06.380
And that works, by the way,
if you want like 500 cores.

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:07.710
Sounds good, you want more?

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:10.890
500 cores, 700 cores,
1,000 cores, go for it.

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:13.640
But when you want 50,000
cores and you want it now,

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:15.143
it's a little different.

00:36:15.143 --> 00:36:17.060
And I think when we start
thinking about that,

00:36:17.060 --> 00:36:18.260
there's two layers.

00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:19.130
The first is people.

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:19.813
All right?

00:36:19.813 --> 00:36:21.980
We want to give you all the
capacity we can do, just

00:36:21.980 --> 00:36:23.282
to support your activities.

00:36:23.282 --> 00:36:24.740
But there are many
other people who

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:27.680
also want 50,000 cores of the
same thing that you might.

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:30.290
So, thinking about
that idea, you

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:33.380
start to realize that, wow,
it's not just 50,000 for me.

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:36.500
It's 50,000 for a lots
of other large customers.

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:39.290
And then also it's kind of
reasonable to say that hardware

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:43.280
is still a thing that we need
to buy and put in our data

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:43.860
centers.

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:44.693
And that takes time.

00:36:44.693 --> 00:36:46.152
That was outlined
here, too, right?

00:36:46.152 --> 00:36:48.890
At the end of the day, Cloud is
just somebody else's computer.

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:50.310
We need more computers.

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:55.280
So infinite capacity makes
sense, but at the right scale,

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:57.730
rather than at a massive one.

00:36:57.730 --> 00:37:01.430
KEYUR GOVANDE: And
Danny and other Googlers

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:03.920
were good partners to
help us figure out like,

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:07.100
how much heads-up do we need to
give them if we needed 50,000

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:09.140
cores when we needed them.

00:37:09.140 --> 00:37:12.320
So this was something
that we had to learn.

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:14.780
Our cycle was a year before.

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:17.570
Now it was much smaller,
we could give them

00:37:17.570 --> 00:37:19.180
a heads-up maybe
a month in advance

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:21.388
and we would have the
hardware, rather than 12 months

00:37:21.388 --> 00:37:24.390
in advance as it was before.

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:27.660
So coming to an end,
circles of success.

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:29.300
DANNY ROSEN: Yeah, so I think--

00:37:29.300 --> 00:37:32.990
we added this slide in because
we wanted to call out, I think,

00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:35.590
one of the fundamental factors
of a large scale migration

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:37.010
of a system like this.

00:37:37.010 --> 00:37:38.510
That it takes a village.

00:37:38.510 --> 00:37:40.940
So there were Googlers, a
tremendous amount of Googlers,

00:37:40.940 --> 00:37:43.550
who put in their time and effort
to make sure this happened.

00:37:43.550 --> 00:37:45.380
And it happened
pretty seamlessly,

00:37:45.380 --> 00:37:48.020
not just for Google, but really
for Etsy and Etsy's customers.

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:49.738
And then there's the Etsyans.

00:37:49.738 --> 00:37:51.530
Now what kind of
interesting is, you really

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:52.910
have these three circles.

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:55.340
And I'll attribute this to
Chris, who's in the front row

00:37:55.340 --> 00:37:56.820
here, he came up with this idea.

00:37:56.820 --> 00:38:00.160
Which was, there are
three circles of influence

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:01.400
or ownership or impact--

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:04.220
we'll call it three
circles of impact in Etsy.

00:38:04.220 --> 00:38:06.140
The first is kind of
your core team, right.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:08.180
This is your squad.

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:10.490
Not only are these people
there to develop the systems

00:38:10.490 --> 00:38:13.610
and the ideas around them, but
they're also on-call, right,

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:15.255
because they have
the deepest context.

00:38:15.255 --> 00:38:16.630
So that's the
first kind of core.

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:19.220
And that's like maybe
five, seven people or so.

00:38:19.220 --> 00:38:21.595
And then outside of
that, as Keyur mentioned,

00:38:21.595 --> 00:38:23.720
you had a team of people
who were testing the site.

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:25.850
Like going to etsy.com,
when it was live at 4:00

00:38:25.850 --> 00:38:28.628
in the morning, and making sure
that the manual testing covered

00:38:28.628 --> 00:38:30.170
all the edge cases
and use cases they

00:38:30.170 --> 00:38:31.640
didn't think of in the
first place, right.

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:33.348
These people are giving
up a lot of time.

00:38:33.348 --> 00:38:35.030
They're coming
[INAUDIBLE] beforehand.

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:37.290
And they're there to
help and make an impact.

00:38:37.290 --> 00:38:38.723
So this is kind
of another layer.

00:38:38.723 --> 00:38:40.140
And then finally,
outside of that,

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:42.740
you have this broad layer of
people in the organization that

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:43.930
supported everyone.

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:45.530
You had management,
you had teammates

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:47.520
who took on work that
other folks would say,

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:49.812
"Hey, listen, I can't do this
because I'm on the squad.

00:38:49.812 --> 00:38:51.830
Can you help me
out," even people

00:38:51.830 --> 00:38:53.780
in security who
are on the outside,

00:38:53.780 --> 00:38:57.110
actually letting people into
the building at 3:00 AM.

00:38:57.110 --> 00:38:57.980
It takes a village.

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:00.890
And I think it's worth
saying that and acknowledging

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:04.970
that a Cloud migration, really
any systems migration to Cloud,

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:07.940
is best done as a team.

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:09.695
And, notably, value
should be attributed

00:39:09.695 --> 00:39:10.820
to the people who did that.

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:11.450
All right?

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:14.033
So even the security person who
woke up at 2:30 in the morning

00:39:14.033 --> 00:39:15.710
to get to the
Brooklyn office should

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:18.293
be given credit because he was
there also to make that happen.

00:39:20.208 --> 00:39:21.250
KEYUR GOVANDE: All right.

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:26.410
So, as I said speaking before,
they did a lift and shift,

00:39:26.410 --> 00:39:28.582
but it is not where
we want to end up.

00:39:28.582 --> 00:39:30.790
We did a lift and shift for
a bunch of reasons, which

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:33.050
was to move fast and
to move in confidence

00:39:33.050 --> 00:39:36.670
and to introduce less
risk into what was already

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:38.770
a fairly complex migration.

00:39:38.770 --> 00:39:41.150
But we don't want to
rest on our laurels,

00:39:41.150 --> 00:39:42.440
we don't want to stay there.

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:44.300
We want to make things better.

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:47.110
So we want to be
refactoring our monolith

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:50.660
to fit better in the Cloud, to
use Cloud-native technologies.

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:54.550
We also want to get a good
handle on cost management.

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:56.830
Being in the data
center, the cost equation

00:39:56.830 --> 00:39:58.160
was relatively straightforward.

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:00.580
It was, you buy some
hardware and then you

00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:02.710
use it for three years,
or you don't use it,

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:03.850
but it doesn't matter.

00:40:03.850 --> 00:40:05.620
Whereas in GCP,
it really matters

00:40:05.620 --> 00:40:08.650
if the box is up and running
and you're not using it.

00:40:08.650 --> 00:40:10.120
And we have a lot
of technical debt

00:40:10.120 --> 00:40:13.270
that we need to pay down,
like not a lot of it--

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:16.750
I mean, some of it added because
of the migration but some of it

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:20.200
that existed way before, that
we feel that we can now pay down

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:21.650
much more effectively.

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:26.140
For example, if we wanted to
spin up new servers in the data

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:28.930
center, we would have to
follow the usual Waterfall

00:40:28.930 --> 00:40:31.630
Model of placing those
orders early enough so

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:34.930
that we were able to have
the capacity needed to effect

00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:36.370
any kind of refactoring.

00:40:36.370 --> 00:40:38.240
In GCP it's a lot
more straightforward.

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:40.210
You spin up some
Terraform config,

00:40:40.210 --> 00:40:42.490
as long as Google has the
heads up about the capacity

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:44.800
that we need, it's
there and were

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:49.150
able to execute and iterate
much more quickly and with less

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:50.860
valence friction.

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:56.260
And also look at
our org structures.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:57.800
We're running in the Cloud.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:01.390
SRE is a thing that we want to
adopt, the practices around it,

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:03.430
around building reliable
systems in the Cloud,

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:06.700
that we did not necessarily
need to focus on when

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:08.630
we were in the data center.

00:41:08.630 --> 00:41:11.408
So some of the things that
Etsy's working on now that

00:41:11.408 --> 00:41:11.950
were past it.

00:41:11.950 --> 00:41:15.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

